:: Day1_Summons_at_Dawn [warrior_queen_arc day1]

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
/*  THE BLADE REFORGED — Day 1: The Summons at Dawn           */
/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* ── SCENE 1: THE WAKING ── */

The knock cometh not as a knock at all, but as a fist upon oak — three strikes, heavy as a headsman's count, splitting the black silence of my chamber like an axe through kindling.

I jolt upright in bed. The sheets pool at my waist, and for one disoriented heartbeat I feel them drag across my chest in a way that sendeth a faint, prickling shiver through me — across skin that hath grown tender these past weeks, across the small swellings beneath my nightgown that I have trained myself not to look at, not to touch, not to think upon.

I think upon them anyway.

The knock cometh again.

<<speech "aldric">>Open this door, Your Grace. Or I shall open it myself, and it shall not enjoy the experience.<</speech>>

That voice. Gravel dragged across iron. I know it as well as I know the sound of my own breathing — though even that hath changed of late. Shallower. Quicker. As though my lungs have forgotten how much room they once commanded.

I swing my legs from the bed and my bare feet find the cold stone floor. The nightgown — a thing of pale linen that Veletia had practically forced upon me three nights past, declaring my old sleeping trousers *"an insult to the very concept of rest"* — falleth to my mid-thigh. I tug at it as I cross the chamber. It clingeth to places it ought not cling. The fabric brusheth against the slight swell of my chest with every step, a whisper of sensation I cannot silence.

I open the door.

Sir Aldric Vornhelm filleth the doorframe the way a boulder filleth a riverbed — immovably, and with the quiet promise that anything foolish enough to contest the arrangement shall be ground to dust. His scarred face is lit by the single corridor torch behind him, one half in amber light, the other in shadow. His arms are crossed over a chest that could serve as a battering ram. Frostfang hangeth at his hip, the great broadsword's pommel catching torchlight like a baleful eye.

He looketh at me.

I become acutely, miserably aware that I am standing before the Commander of the Royal Guard in a woman's nightgown, barefoot, with my hair — longer now, past my jaw, unruly with sleep — falling across one eye.

His gaze traveleth downward for precisely one second. Then back up. His expression doth not change. But something in his jaw tighteneth, the way a man's jaw tighteneth when he hath bitten into something unexpected and is deciding whether to swallow or spit.

<<speech "aldric">>Get dressed. Training clothes. Iron Garden. You have the time it taketh me to walk there.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric, the sun hath not even—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>The sun riseth when it pleaseth. I rise when the kingdom demandeth it. So shalt thou.<</speech>>

He turneth on his heel and marcheth down the corridor, his boots striking the stone in a rhythm as precise and unforgiving as a war drum.

I stand in the doorway, the cold air raising gooseflesh along my bare legs — legs that are thinner than they were a month ago, smoother, the muscle beneath them leaner and longer. I glance down. The nightgown's hem swayeth against my thighs. My thighs, which now press together at the top when I stand with my feet close, a soft contact that was not there before, a warmth between them that is new and strange and impossible to ignore when one is standing half-naked in a cold corridor at an ungodly hour.

I close the door and lean against it.

My heart is hammering. Not from fear of Aldric — though only a fool would not fear Aldric — but from the look in his eyes. That single downward glance. He had *seen* it. The changes. The narrowing of my shoulders, the soft beginning of curve at my waist, the way the nightgown draped over a silhouette that no longer matcheth the one he hath trained for ten years.

He had seen it, and he had said nothing, and somehow that is worse than any words.


/* ── SCENE 2: DRESSING ── */

I cross to the wardrobe and pull it open. The left side holdeth my old clothes — tunics and breeches cut for a prince's frame. The right side, added only recently by Veletia's insistence, holdeth newer garments. Training clothes that have been... adjusted. I reach for a familiar tunic and pull it on.

It doth not fit.

The shoulders sag where once they filled. The chest hangeth loose everywhere except— there. Two small points where the fabric catcheth and pulleth taut, outlining what is growing beneath with a clarity that maketh my stomach drop. I cross my arms over my chest instinctively, pressing the fabric flat, but the moment I release, it springeth back to its traitorous shape.

I tear it off and reach for one of the newer tunics — a darker fabric, thicker weave, with a slightly gathered front that Veletia had designed to *"accommodate the journey,"* as she had put it with that infuriating theatrical wink.

It fitteth better. The chest is... handled. Not hidden, precisely, but managed. The waist is tapered. When I look in the small mirror beside the wardrobe, the figure that stares back at me is...

I look away.

Breeches next. I pull them on and — they are tight at the hip. Tighter than last week. I have to tug and shift, and when I finally fasten them, the fabric stretcheth across a pelvis that hath widened by a degree I can feel in my very bones, a spreading ache deep in the joint that Thalara telleth me is *"the cradle reshaping itself."*

The cradle. As though my body is making room for something I cannot bring myself to name.

I lace my boots. Even these feel different — my feet, Thalara said, have lost perhaps half a size. The boots are slightly loose. I stuff a bit of cloth into the toes as I have taken to doing and curse softly.

I catch my reflection one final time as I move toward the door. The figure in the mirror is lean, angular still, but softened at every edge — like a blade that someone hath begun to grind into a different shape. The jaw. The neck. The way the tunic falleth. The flare at the hip where once there was a straight descent from rib to thigh.

I look like a boy wearing a woman's bones.

Or a woman wearing a boy's stubbornness.

I leave the mirror to its judgments and step into the corridor.


/* ── SCENE 3: THE WALK TO THE IRON GARDEN ── */

The castle is dark and silent at this hour. My boots echo against stone — a sound that is lighter than it used to be. I weigh less. My stride is shorter. I catch myself walking with my elbows closer to my body, my steps more contained, as though my frame is instinctively folding inward, occupying less space.

When did I start walking like this?

I pass the great corridor where the ancestors' portraits hang. Queens, all of them. Velmorian queens stretching back seven hundred years — armoured, crowned, fierce-eyed women who ruled with blade and blood. I have walked past these portraits ten thousand times. But this morning, for the first time, I do not see them as ancestors.

I see them as mirrors.

My reflection flasheth across the glass of Queen Elyria's portrait as I pass. For one terrible, tilting instant, I see a young woman in the glass — dark-haired, uncertain, her body caught between two truths — and I cannot tell where Elyria endeth and I begin.

I walk faster.

The Iron Garden is the castle's oldest training ground — a walled yard of packed earth and stone, ringed by weapon racks and scarred wooden dummies that have absorbed the fury of a hundred generations of Velmorian warriors. The morning air biteth at my skin as I step through the arch. The sky is that bruised blue-grey that precedeth the dawn, and the stars are fading like sparks dying on an anvil.

Aldric is already there. Of course he is.

He standeth in the centre of the yard with his arms behind his back, Frostfang unsheathed and planted point-down in the earth before him. He hath the stillness of a man who hath been waiting a long time and intendeth to make someone pay for it.

<<speech "aldric">>Thou art late.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>You gave me the time it took you to walk here.<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Aye. And thou tookest longer. That is what late meaneth.<</speech>>

I bite back a retort and walk to the centre of the yard to stand before him. The morning cold is sharper than I expected. My new skin — thinner, softer, more susceptible to temperature, as I have been learning in a hundred small miserable ways — prickleth with gooseflesh beneath the tunic. I feel my nipples tighten against the fabric and I pray to every god in every temple that Aldric cannot see them through the weave.

He is not looking at my chest. He is looking at all of me, and that is far worse.


/* ── SCENE 4: THE ASSESSMENT ── */

Aldric pulleth Frostfang from the earth and sheathe it in one fluid motion. Then he walketh toward the weapon rack, selecteth a standard training longsword, and tosseth it to me.

I catch it.

Or rather — I try to catch it. The sword spineth once in the air and my hand closeth around the grip, but the weight pulleth my arm down farther than I expect. It is the same training sword I have wielded since I was fourteen. It weigheth the same it hath always weighed. But my arm doth not.

<<speech "aldric">>Swing it. Basic overhead, on my count.<</speech>>

I set my feet in the wide stance I have used for a decade — feet shoulder-width apart, weight evenly distributed, blade high. Aldric raiseth a hand.

<<speech "aldric">>One.<</speech>>

I bring the blade down. The arc is clean, practiced—

— and my right hip *shifteth*. The wider pelvis, the altered balance point, the subtle redistribution of weight that the potions have wrought — it all conspireth in one treacherous instant. My centre of gravity, which hath sat in my solar plexus for twenty years, hath dropped. Moved lower. Settled somewhere in my gut, maybe lower still. The swing that should have been straight pulleth to the left, my hips rotating in a way I did not command, and my back foot skiddeth on the packed earth.

I stumble. Not a fall — I catch myself — but a graceless, lurching half-step that would have been fatal against any opponent faster than a sleeping cow.

Aldric watcheth. His expression is stone.

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

I reset. Plant my feet. Swing.

The same thing. The hip. The rotation. The pull to the left. This time I overcorrect and my upper body leaneth too far right, and the weight of the sword — gods, why is it so *heavy* — drags me into a full stumble. My knee hiteth the dirt.

<<speech "aldric">>Again.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Aldric, something is—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I know precisely what is. Again.<</speech>>

I rise. I swing. The sword goeth wide. My shoulders — narrower by two fingers' breadth this month — cannot generate the same rotational force. The torque that once came from broad, thick trapezius muscles and a wide collarbone now meeteth a frame that is being whittled thinner by the day. The power isn't there. Not the way it was.

I swing again. And again. Each time, something is wrong. The balance. The power. The angle. My body is a house whose foundations have shifted in the night, and every wall hangeth at an angle that looketh right but is imperceptibly, catastrophically wrong.

After the tenth swing, Aldric raiseth his hand.

<<speech "aldric">>Stop.<</speech>>

I stand there, breathing hard — harder than ten swings should warrant, but my lungs are shallower now, my ribcage subtly compressed, and the air cometh in quick, insufficient gulps. Sweat hath already begun to bead at my temples. The tunic clingeth to my back, and at my chest, the dampness outlineth the two small mounds with a fidelity that maketh me want to rip the fabric off and fling it into the nearest fire.

Aldric approacheth. He stoppeth an arm's length away and looketh at me the way a mason looketh at a wall that hath cracked — not with anger, but with the cold calculation of a man deciding how much must be torn down before rebuilding can begin.

<<speech "aldric">>How long since thy last potion?<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>Four days. Thalara administered the torso draught on—<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I did not ask for a calendar. I asked because I needed to know how much ground I've lost. Four days. The torso draught.<</speech>>

He walketh around me. Slowly. His boots crunch on the packed earth and I feel his gaze like a physical thing — tracking the line of my shoulders, the set of my spine, the flare of my hips beneath the breeches.

<<speech "aldric">>Thy shoulders have dropped by two fingers since I last measured thee. Thy arms are thinner — not weaker yet, but the mass is redistributing. Thy waist hath drawn in. I can see it even through that tunic, and I suspect thou can feel it every time thou breathest.<</speech>>

I say nothing. He is not wrong.

<<speech "aldric">>And thy hips—<</speech>>

He pauseth behind me. I can feel him looking at the change that is, perhaps, the most visible of all — the widening of my pelvis, the way my breeches strain at the sides, the new breadth that throweth every stance, every step, every movement into disarray.

<<speech "aldric">>Thy centre of gravity hath dropped three inches at the least. Thy old stance — the wide warrior's plant — it is not merely ineffective now. It is dangerous. Thy body will fight thee every time thou triest to use it. That stumble just now? In a real fight, that is a sword through thy gut.<</speech>>

He cometh back around to face me. His scarred face is grim, but there is something else in his eyes — not pity, never pity from Aldric — but a kind of severe, reluctant respect for the magnitude of what I am enduring.

<<speech "aldric">>Everything I have taught thee. Every stance. Every guard. Every strike pattern, every footwork drill, every instinct I have beaten into thy muscle over ten years of training. It is all wrong now. Every last piece of it. Not because thou hast forgotten, but because the body that learned it no longer existeth.<</speech>>

The words land like blows. I feel them in my chest — in the new, tender flesh that acheth when struck, in the ribs that are subtly reshaping themselves, in the heart that beateth faster and lighter than a prince's heart ought.

<<speech "mc">>Then what am I to do?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Thou art to unlearn. And then thou art to learn again. From the first step. From the first breath. As though thou hast never held a blade in thy life.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>I have trained since I was ten years old. You would have me begin as a child?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>I would have thee begin as what thou art. Which is neither the boy I trained nor the woman thou art becoming, but something in between — something that doth not yet know its own strength, its own reach, its own limits. And that, Your Grace, is the most dangerous thing a warrior can be.<</speech>>

He letteth the silence sit for a moment. The first pale fingers of dawn have begun to creep over the eastern wall, painting the tops of the weapon racks in thin gold.

<<speech "aldric">>I am calling it The Reforging. Starting on the morrow, thou wilt report to this yard at first light. Every day. No exceptions. Not illness, not council, not feast, not the gods-damned end of the world. We will take apart everything thou thinkest thou knowest about fighting and we will rebuild it — for the body thou hast. Not the body thou had. Not the body thou wilt have. The one thou standest in, right now, today, this hour.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>And if my body changeth again tomorrow? If the next potion reshapeth me further?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Then we adjust. A blade must be reforged each time the steel is reworked. That is the nature of what thou art doing to thyself. Thou hast chosen to melt down and recast thy very form, and I — I must teach a shifting blade how to cut true. It is...<</speech>>

He pauseth. For a moment, something almost human flickereth behind the iron mask of his face.

<<speech "aldric">>It is the most difficult thing I have ever been asked to do. And I have been asked to do many things by many queens. But I will do it. Because if I do not, the next assassin, the next battle, the next creature that cometh out of the dark will find thee with a body thou dost not understand and a fighting style built for a ghost — and thou wilt die. And Velmoria will die with thee.<</speech>>

I stare at him. The weight of it — the training, the transformation, the kingdom balanced upon my changing shoulders — presseth down upon me like a physical force. I feel it in the balls of my feet, in the ache of my widened hips, in the tight trembling of muscles that are no longer shaped for the burdens they carry.

<<speech "mc">>Every day? First light?<</speech>>

<<speech "aldric">>Every day. First light. And thou wilt wear what I tell thee to wear. Train how I tell thee to train. Fight how I tell thee to fight. No arguments. No pride. The prince is dead on this training ground, Your Grace. Only the student remaineth.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>And what of the queen? Is she dead too?<</speech>>

He looketh at me for a long moment. Then, so faintly I might have imagined it:

<<speech "aldric">>The queen hath not been born yet. That is what the Reforging is for.<</speech>>

He turneth toward the armoury gate, pausing without looking back.

<<speech "aldric">>First light. Do not make me come to thy chamber again. I'll not be so gentle next time.<</speech>>

<<speech "mc">>That was gentle?<</speech>>

But he is already gone, his silhouette swallowed by the archway, and the sound of his boots fadeth into the grey corridors of the castle.


/* ── SCENE 5: THE RETURN TO THE CHAMBER ── */

I walk back to my chamber slowly. The castle is beginning to stir — servants moving through the lower corridors, the distant clang of the kitchen, the creak of doors opening onto the day. I pass a pair of chambermaids carrying linens. They curtsy and murmur *"Your Grace"* — but their eyes linger. On my hair. On the shape of me beneath the tunic. On the way I move.

I walk differently now and I cannot stop it. My hips sway — just slightly, just enough — with each step. A gentle lateral motion that I did not learn and cannot unlearn, born of the changed architecture of my pelvis, the altered attachment of muscle to bone. When I was a prince, I walked with my shoulders. Now something deeper driveth my gait, something seated low in the body, and every step is a tiny confession that I cannot take back.

Inside my chamber, I close the door and lean against it. The fire hath died to embers. The room is cold. I pull off the boots, the breeches, the tunic, and stand in naught but smallclothes — the newer ones, the ones Veletia had made, softer fabric, slightly different cut, designed for a shape that is no longer entirely—

I stop undressing. I am standing in the middle of my chamber in women's underclothes, shivering, and outside the window the sky is bleeding pink and gold, and somewhere in this castle Sir Aldric is already planning how to tear me apart and reassemble me as something useful, something deadly, something worthy of a throne I am not sure I want and a body I did not choose.

I sit upon the edge of my bed.

The mattress dippeth beneath me and I feel my thighs press together — that soft, warm contact at the top, flesh against flesh, where once there was a gap. I press my palms against the outside of my thighs and feel the new breadth of them, the way they curve outward from a waist that is pulling inward like a drawn breath.

The nightgown lieth where I left it, crumpled upon the pillow. I pick it up. The linen is still warm from my body. It smelleth of me — but even that is changing. Softer. Less musk, more... something floral, almost. As though the alchemy hath reached even my sweat, my skin's own perfume.

I press the nightgown against my face and breathe.

<<speech "mc">>First light. Every day.<</speech>>

I say it aloud to the empty room, to the dying fire, to the body that is betraying and remaking me in equal measure. I say it like a soldier reciting an oath before battle. Because that is what this is. A war — not against an enemy, but against the distance between who I was and who I must become.

I fold the nightgown and place it on the pillow. I pull the blanket up to my chin. The fabric slideth across my chest — across the tender, swelling flesh that acheth faintly with each heartbeat, that presseth against whatever I wear, that remindeth me with every breath that the boy who once slept in this bed is becoming someone else.

Sleep doth not return.

But somewhere in the darkness, between one heartbeat and the next, I hear Aldric's voice again:

*The queen hath not been born yet.*

I lie still. I feel the alchemy working in my bones — a deep, tidal pull, patient and relentless, reshaping me cell by cell while the world sleepeth.

The queen hath not been born yet.

But she is stirring.

And come first light, she will learn to fight.


/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
/*  END OF DAY 1                                               */
